      "timestamp_raw": "Nov 3, 2022 20:39:50 GMT -5",
      "body_html": "some more shots of Slurricane creek white....err rather Hurricane creek white clone F, pics taken mostly on 10/1/22.  They were already starting to get a bit beat up by the time I had a chance to photograph them, but hey, better late than never:<br/><img src=\"https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52476050395_f30c894f74_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52476050265_bbbb2ac921_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/>AT least outdoors in northern california, it's completely normal for older traps to eventually get totally messed up, this has happened every year for the past 20 years.  They are just starting to get the punching bag treatment in these pics, but some traps are still pristine:<br/><img src=\"https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52475866414_418fd62c26_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52476049840_b0b4518e7e_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52475865904_d8abd5c562_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/><br/><br/><img src=\"https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52475085787_14138592f0_z.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/>",
